# NCHECK(1M)

## NAME

ncheck - generate names from i-numbers

## SYNOPSIS

| **/etc/ncheck** [ **-i** *numbers* ] [ **-a** ] [ **-s** ] [ *filesystem* ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Ncheck* with no argument generates a pathname vs. i-number list of all files on **/dev/rmd0**.
Names of directory files are followed by `/.`. The **-i** option reduces the report to only
those files whose i-numbers follow; up to a hundred may be given. The **-a** option allows
printing of the names `.` and `..`, which are ordinarily suppressed. The **-s** option reduces
the report to special files and files with set-user-ID mode; it is intended to discover
concealed violations of security policy.

A file system may be specified.

The report is in no useful order, and probably should be sorted.

**Note:** *quot*(1M)'s **-n** option reads exactly this report on its standard input, so

> **ncheck /dev/rmd0 | sort +0n | quot -n /dev/rmd0**

names every file on the volume with the owner and size of the i-node it belongs to. That was
*quot*'s reason for having the option and this program is what it was waiting for.

**Note:** a name is printed to **eighteen** characters. *DIRSIZ* is 18 on this machine rather
than v7's 14, a directory entry being four 48-bit words - one of i-number and three of name. A
name carries eight bits per byte and is not masked, so a Cyrillic name comes back as it was
written.

**Note:** v7 held its directories in a fixed hash table of 2503 entries and gave up with `out
of core -- increase HSIZE` when it filled. The table here is allocated from the heap and sized
from the super-block's own i-list, so there is no fixed ceiling and no partial report: either
the whole volume can be walked or the program refuses at the outset.

## FILES

/dev/rmd0

## SEE ALSO

dcheck(1M), icheck(1M), clri(1M), fsck(1M), quot(1M), sort(1), filsys(5)

## DIAGNOSTICS

When the filesystem structure is improper, `???` denotes the `parent` of a parentless file and
a pathname beginning with `...` denotes a loop.

`filesystem: not a filesystem` means the super-block carries no *FS_MAGIC* and `filesystem
geometry mismatch` that it describes a block size, an i-nodes-per-block or an
addresses-per-inode this system does not have. **Note:** v7 had neither check, having no such
fields.

## BUGS

Since *ncheck* walks the i-list three times, extraneous names may be produced if applied to
active file systems.
